From 1b7263e16b7c4c3971a2ff733f4da46dd28facfc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Silas=20M=C3=BCller?= <dev@silasmueller.de>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 15:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] vmm: report a PCI segment's DMA translation in ACPI _DMA

A PCI host bridge may translate the addresses its devices put on the bus
before they reach memory. ACPI describes this with the bridge's _DMA
object (ACPI 6.5 sec 6.2.4): a resource template, in _CRS format, of the
child-side ranges the bridge forwards, each with an Address Translation
Offset _TRA that is added to the child-side (bus) address to obtain the
parent-side (system physical) address (sec 6.4.3.5.1). Cloud Hypervisor
emits _CCA and _CRS for every PCI segment's host bridge but never _DMA,
so a guest always assumes bus address == guest physical address.

That identity does not hold for a device whose DMA the VMM does not
perform itself and cannot route through a vIOMMU, for example a
vfio-user or vhost-user device backed by hardware that sits in a host
IOMMU domain whose I/O virtual addresses are allocated by a host driver.
The backend then knows the bus address at which each piece of guest RAM
is visible to the hardware, and the guest has to use those addresses.

Add an optional per-segment table:

    --pci-segment pci_segment=<id>,dma_ranges=[<cpu_start>:<dma_start>:<size>,...]

Each entry says that a device on the segment reaches guest physical
address cpu_start + n at bus address dma_start + n, for n < size. Numbers
are decimal or 0x-prefixed hex. When the table is present the segment's
PNP0A08 device carries

    Name (_DMA, ResourceTemplate () {
        QWordMemory (ResourceProducer, PosDecode, MinFixed, MaxFixed,
                     Cacheable, ReadWrite, 0,
                     dma_start, dma_start + size - 1,
                     cpu_start - dma_start,      // _TRA, two's complement
                     size)
        ...
    })

one descriptor per entry. Without the option nothing changes: no _DMA
is emitted and the DSDT is byte-identical.

The option lives on --pci-segment because _DMA is defined only for
devices that represent buses (sec 6.2.4), so a segment's host bridge is
the finest granularity the specification offers; a device that needs a
translation that other devices must not see is placed on a segment of
its own (--platform num_pci_segments=N and pci_segment= on the device).
A VM-wide --platform option would force the translation on every device.

The configuration is validated: every range must be non-empty, 4 KiB
aligned and not wrap, and no two ranges may overlap on the CPU side or
on the bus side, so the translation is unambiguous in both directions.
A bus-side range that overlaps the segment's own MMIO windows is only
warned about: the specification treats such child-side accesses as
peer-to-peer, but the VMM cannot know whether the backend's hardware
does.

Consumers: Linux evaluates _DMA through acpi_dma_get_range() in
drivers/acpi/scan.c and installs the result as the device's
dma_range_map; arm64 and LoongArch do this today from
acpi_arch_dma_setup(). x86 does not call it yet, so on x86 guests the
object is inert until the guest kernel gains the same hook.

Tests: config parsing and validation, and the exact AML bytes of the
_DMA object for a two-range table.

